**Exact-length invariant.** A matrix of length p stores the optimum for exactly p real edges between every ordered pair, or `None`. The identity represents exactly zero edges, only between equal vertices; the initial power represents exactly one real edge, selecting the cheapest parallel edge. Multiplication of lengths p and q computes

C[a,b] = min over v of (A[a,v] + B[v,b]).

Every p+q-edge walk splits after p edges at some v. Its halves cost at least the corresponding optima; conversely, optimal finite halves concatenate to attain their sum. Therefore multiplication preserves optimal cost and exact length. During exponentiation, `result` represents the processed binary contribution and `power` represents the current power-of-two length; termination gives exactly L.

**Witness correctness.** Every concatenation references previously created nodes, so the representation is acyclic. Its left endpoint is the left child's start, its right endpoint is the right child's end, and the children's meeting endpoints agree. Lengths and costs add. Induction therefore proves directed continuity, exactly L real edges, and the reported cost. Empty nodes add no edge; real self-loops do add one. Parallel edges remain distinguishable by their original IDs. For L = 0, only S = T succeeds.

**Retrieval.** An index below the left length belongs to the left child; otherwise subtract that length and descend right. This maintains the index within the chosen child's represented walk until reaching a real-edge leaf. Squared-power witnesses have depth at most floor(log₂ L), and accumulated-result concatenations add at most one level per set bit, so retrieval takes O(log(L + 1)) time and O(1) extra stored integers.

**Deterministic bounds.** Put b = ceil(log₂(L + 1)). There are O(b) matrix multiplications, each scanning N³ candidates and creating at most N² concatenation nodes. Construction takes O(M + N² + N³b) unit-cost time and stores O(M + N²(1 + b)) integers, including superseded initial leaves; the returned DAG alone satisfies the same bound. These are worst-case bounds, not heuristic expectations. No walk expansion occurs.

With the stated limits, every represented length is at most L, and every cost has magnitude at most L·10¹² ≤ 10³⁰ < 2¹⁰⁰. Thus exact Python arithmetic uses a bounded number of integer digits. More generally, arithmetic costs must be charged according to those integer bit lengths. A negative self-loop may make the optimum very negative, but fixed L permits only finitely many edge sequences, so every reachable exact-length optimum remains finite.

