Save as `exact_root.py` and run `python3 exact_root.py`. No dependencies are required. Call `witness.edge_at(i)` to obtain an input edge ID without materializing earlier edges.

**Correctness.** A matrix representing length a stores, at entry (u,v), a witness for the minimum-cost u-to-v walk using exactly a edges, or `None` if impossible. The identity establishes this invariant for zero edges, and the initial power establishes it for one edge by selecting the cheapest actual parallel edge.

In a multiplication, every exactly-(a+b)-edge walk splits at its vertex after a edges. Its cost is at least the corresponding sum of the two optimal entries. Conversely, the two selected witnesses concatenate at that midpoint and attain the sum. Therefore minimizing those sums gives exactly the optimal cost, with exactly a+b edges. Negative values are ordinary costs; only `None` denotes impossibility.

Every concatenation node references previously constructed nodes whose adjoining endpoints agree. Induction proves directed continuity, recorded cost, and recorded length. Empty identity witnesses are omitted during concatenation; real self-loops remain one-edge leaves. Parallel edges retain their original distinct IDs. Binary exponentiation constructs precisely length L, so its final entry proves the returned result.

A fixed-length problem has only finitely many candidate edge sequences. Negative cycles can reduce the optimum but cannot make it unbounded when L is fixed.

**Deterministic bounds.** Let b = L.bit_length(). There are at most b accumulator multiplications and b−1 squarings. Each costs O(N³) and creates at most N² nodes—only after the best midpoint has been selected. Including validation and initialization, construction takes O(M + N² + N³b) integer operations and stores O(M + N²(1+b)) integer fields. For L=0, construction takes O(M + N²).

A power witness for 2ʲ edges has height at most j. Accumulator concatenations add at most b further levels, so any indexed retrieval follows O(b) nodes, using O(1) auxiliary integer fields. No expansion is performed.

All stored costs have magnitude at most L·10¹² ≤ 10³⁰, all lengths are at most 10¹⁸, and node IDs are bounded by the stated construction size. Thus Python integer arithmetic uses a bounded number of digits under these supplied limits. More generally, bit complexity additionally charges for arithmetic on O(log(LW + 1)) bits, where W is the maximum absolute edge weight.
